    paragraphs: [
      'King Bahram Gur wanted to know whether there was really justice in his land. So early one morning he rode out alone on his dark horse Shabdiz, dressed like an ordinary rider, so that nobody would know him. He rode all day, until he was tired.',
      "Near a village he came to a house, and saw a woman coming home with a water jar on her shoulder. 'Can you give a traveller a place to rest?' asked Bahram. And the woman answered, in the Shahnameh's own words: 'O brave rider, treat this house as your own.'",
      'She called her husband, an old gardener, to see to the horse. She swept the room and spread a mat with a cushion. Then she brought bread, yoghurt, fresh greens and a little vinegar. Bahram ate a little and slept. In the evening she cooked a warm supper for her guest.',
      "When night came, Bahram said, 'Tell me a story, good woman. And what do people here say about King Bahram? Have you any complaint about him?'",
      "'The king himself is good,' she said. 'But many riders pass through this village: the king's officials and tax-men. Some of them are unfair. They blame people for things they never did, to squeeze money out of them. That is the trouble we have from the king.'",
      "Bahram lay awake. Her words had stung him. 'Nobody thanks a fair king,' he thought. 'So I will be harsh with my people for a few months, and then they will see what fairness and kindness are worth.' And he fell asleep with that unfair thought in his heart.",
      "At dawn the woman brought the cow in from the pasture, gave her plenty of fresh grass, and sat down to milk her. She said a prayer and stroked the udder. But it was empty. Not one drop. And the woman's heart, young a moment before, felt suddenly old.",
      "'Husband,' she said, 'the king's heart has changed. Last night it turned towards injustice.' 'Why do you say such a thing?' asked the old man. 'I am not talking nonsense,' she said. 'This cow ate the same grass and drank the same water as always. But when a king turns unjust, the milk dries up in the udder.'",
      "Bahram heard every word, and at once he was sorry for his thought. 'O Maker of the world,' he prayed, 'if my heart ever turns away from justice, let me never sit on the throne again.'",
      "The woman stroked the cow again with another prayer, and this time the milk came pouring out! 'The one who turned unjust has turned just again,' she told her husband happily. 'Laugh and be glad, for God has been kind to us.'",
      "She cooked a pot of milk porridge for her guest. Bahram ate some, then said, 'Hang my whip on a high branch of the tree by your door, where the wind won't knock it down, and watch who comes along the road.'",
      "The old man hung up the whip, and before long a great army came down the road. Every soldier who saw it got down and bowed low, praising King Bahram. 'Our guest is the king!' said the husband and wife. Full of shame, they ran to him. 'Great king, we are poor people. Forgive us for such a humble welcome.'",
      "But Bahram smiled. 'I give you this land and this fine village,' he said. 'You need not work as gardeners any more. Just be hosts, always, as you were to me.' Then he laughed, mounted his horse, and rode home.",
    ],
