    paragraphs: [
      'Long ago there was a king of Iran called Anushirvan. He had a wise and cheerful man called Babak, and he gave Babak an important job: Babak was in charge of the muster.',
      "What is a muster? It was the day when every soldier came to show that he, his horse and his weapons were ready. Only then would he get his pay from the king. Babak set up a big, high hall in front of the palace gate and spread a royal carpet on the floor. Then his crier called out: 'Famous warriors! Get on your horses and come, one by one, in helmet and armour, with your maces. Whoever wants his pay from the king, come!'",
      "So the army came. So many riders came that the dust from their horses darkened the sky. Babak looked them over, every one. But he did not see the king's banner, and he did not see the king's crown. So he got up and said, 'Go home, all of you.'",
      "The next morning the call went out again, and again the riders came, with spears and helmets and armour, bows and lassos. Babak looked them over. Still no king. 'Go home again,' he said kindly. 'Go home happy.'",
      "On the third day Babak's crier called out something new: 'Not one rider may stay away from the muster. Every one must come in helmet and armour. And whoever is honoured with a crown and a high throne, let him know this too: at the muster, nobody is let off, and nobody is spoken to gently.'",
      "King Anushirvan heard it. Was he angry? No. He laughed! He called for his helmet, raised his great banner, and rode to Babak's hall. He came in a helmet with chain mail hanging down and armour on his body, a bull-headed mace in his hand, arrows at his waist, a bow on his arm, a lasso on his saddle and a golden belt round his middle. He lifted his heavy mace, turned his horse left and right, and showed Babak every weapon.",
      "Babak looked carefully, and he was pleased. Then he said something very bold. 'Long live the king! Forgive me for being so bold, but please turn your horse once more, to the right, the way a rider as skilled as you should.' And the king turned his horse again, left and right, as quick as fire. Babak stared in wonder.",
      "Then it was time to write down the pay. The best champions in the army got the most pay of all. And Babak wrote down the king's pay: one silver coin more than the best champion's. Then a cry rang out from the hall: 'Bring the horse of the leader of all warriors, the most famous rider in the world: the king!' And Anushirvan laughed and laughed.",
      "Afterwards Babak came to the king. 'Great king,' he said, 'if I was hard on you today, there was only truth and fairness in my heart. Please don't remember me as harsh.'",
      "But the king said, 'Wise man, never step off the path of what is right.' And then, in the poem's words: 'If you hold back to keep yourself safe, you break the heart of truth.' That means: if you go easy on someone so as to stay safe yourself, truth is hurt. 'Today,' said the king, 'your worth has only grown in my eyes.'",
    ],
