    saga: { name: 'shahnameh', order: 240 },
    paragraphs: [
      'Long ago, Khosrow Parviz was king of Iran, and his court was full of music. The king’s chief musician was called Sarkash, and for many years nobody played before the king but Sarkash.',
      'Far away lived another musician, called Barbad. He played the barbat, a lute with a round wooden belly, and when he sang, people stopped to listen. Barbad heard about the king’s court, and he wanted his music to be heard there. So he set off with his barbat.',
      "But when Sarkash heard Barbad play, his heart grew dark. 'He is better than me,' he thought. 'I am growing old, and he is new.' So he went to the king’s gatekeeper and asked him to keep the new musician out. And every time Barbad came to the palace, the door stayed shut.",
      "Barbad did not shout, and he did not give up. He took his barbat and went to the king’s garden. The gardener there was called Mardui, and the two of them became friends that very day. 'Every Nowruz,' said Mardui, 'the king comes to this garden and feasts here for two weeks.' 'Then let me in, my friend,' said Barbad, 'so that I can see the king’s feast, hidden.' 'I will,' said Mardui.",
      'On the day the king was coming, Barbad dressed all in green, from head to toe. He took his barbat and climbed up into a tall cypress tree, green and thick with leaves, close to the place where the king would sit. And there he waited, green in the green, as still as a leaf.',
      "The king came and sat down to his feast. The sun turned yellow, and then the sky grew deep blue. And then, from the cypress, came music: a beautiful song, the one people later called Dad-afarid. Everyone at the feast stopped, amazed. 'Search the garden!' said the king. They searched everywhere. They found nobody.",
      "Then came a second song, the one they called Peykar-e Gord. 'Search again!' said the king. This time they carried lamps under every tree. But they found only willows and cypresses, and pheasants walking under the roses.",
      "Then came a third song, the one they called Sabz dar Sabz, Green in Green. The king stood up. 'If this were an angel,' he said, 'it would be made of musk and amber. And a demon could never sing like that. Find him! I will fill his mouth with jewels and make him chief of all my musicians.'",
      "When Barbad heard that, he climbed down from the cypress and bowed low. 'I am only your servant,' he said, and he told the king the whole story. The king was as happy as a rose garden in spring. He turned to Sarkash. 'You are like a bitter gourd,' he said, 'and Barbad is like sugar. Why did you keep him from me?'",
      'That night the king filled Barbad’s mouth with shining pearls. And from then on, Barbad was the king of musicians, famous among the great.',
    ],
