    paragraphs: [
      "Do you remember Zal, the boy with snow-white hair who grew up in the Simorgh's nest? Zal became a man and married Rudabeh, the brave and lovely princess of Kabul, and before long a baby was on the way.",
      "But this was no ordinary baby. He grew and grew, until he was so big and so heavy that Rudabeh could hardly move. Her rosy cheeks turned as pale as saffron. 'Mother,' she said to Sindokht, 'I feel as if I were full of stones, or full of iron.' Day and night she could not rest.",
      'The time came for the baby to be born, and still he could not come out. One day Rudabeh was so tired that she fainted, and her mother cried out for help. Zal came running. He sat beside his wife, and the tears ran down his face.',
      "Then, all at once, he remembered. The feather! The Simorgh's feather, which he had kept safe all those years. Zal smiled through his tears. 'Don't be afraid,' he told Sindokht. 'Help is coming.' He brought a little dish of fire and burned a tiny piece of the feather.",
      "At once the sky grew dark, and there was the Simorgh, like a great cloud. Zal bowed low and thanked her. 'Why are there tears in a lion's eyes?' said the Simorgh. 'Don't cry. A child is coming who will be as tall as a cypress and as strong as an elephant.'",
      "Then she told Zal what to do. 'Bring a wise and skilful healer. He will help Rudabeh fall into a deep sleep, so that she feels no pain, and while she sleeps, he will bring the baby out safe and sound. Then crush the herb I tell you of with milk and musk, lay it on her, and stroke her with my feather, and she will heal. And thank God for this child.' She gave Zal a feather from her wing and flew away.",
      "Everything was done just as the Simorgh said. The healer came, Rudabeh slept, and the baby was brought out safe and well. What a baby! So big and strong that everyone gasped. The healing herb did its work, and Rudabeh's pain went away.",
      "Rudabeh slept for a whole day and a whole night. When she woke, they brought her the baby. She looked at him and laughed. 'Barastam!' she said. 'I am free! My sorrow is over!' And so they named the boy Rostam. As the poem says: 'Barastam, she said, my sorrow is done; and Rostam they named the boy.'",
      "Grandpa Sam was far away, so they made him a present: a silk doll, just as big as baby Rostam, stuffed with soft sable fur. On its cheeks they painted the sun and the planet Venus. They put a tiny mace in one hand and reins in the other, sat it on a horse, and sent it to Grandpa Sam.",
      "When Sam saw the silk doll, his hair stood on end with joy. 'This baby looks just like me!' he cried. He wrote back a happy letter. 'Look after this child,' he said, 'so that not even a breath of wind can harm him.'",
      'And baby Rostam grew. Ten nurses fed him, and when he was old enough for bread and meat, he ate as much as five grown men! By the age of eight, he was as tall as a cypress tree.',
    ],
