    paragraphs: [
      'In the land of Turan a boy was born, named Kay Khosrow. His mother was Farangis, daughter of Afrasiab, king of Turan. His father was Siavash, a prince of Iran, but his father was no longer alive. And Afrasiab, his own grandfather, was afraid of him, for wise men had said a great king would come from this child.',
      "So the king told Piran, a kind lord of Turan, 'Send the boy to the shepherds in the mountains, where he will never learn who he is.' Piran took him to the shepherds of Mount Qala, with gifts and a nurse. 'Look after this child like your own soul,' he said.",
      "At seven, he made himself a bow from a stick and a string of gut, and an arrow with no feathers and no point, and went hunting. By ten, he faced bears and wolves, and even lions and leopards, with that same bent stick. The shepherd hurried to Piran, worried: 'If anything happens to this boy, you will blame me!'",
      "Piran laughed. 'Noble birth and courage cannot stay hidden.' He rode up and hugged the boy for a long time. 'You hug a shepherd's son,' said Kay Khosrow, 'and you are not ashamed?' 'You are the crown of kings,' said Piran. 'Who says you are a shepherd's son?' And he took him home to his own house.",
      "One dark night the king sent for Piran. 'I cannot sleep for thinking of that child. Bring him.' 'He is only a boy who knows nothing,' said Piran. 'But first, promise me, as kings promise, that you will not harm him.' And Afrasiab swore a great oath.",
      "Piran told the boy, 'Put your good sense away for today. Act a little silly.' When Afrasiab asked about the sheep, the boy said, 'There is nothing to hunt, and I have no bow and arrows.' Asked about his teacher, he talked about leopards. Asked about his mother and father, he talked about lions and dogs.",
      "Afrasiab laughed. 'Ask him about his head, and he answers about his feet! This boy will do no harm. Send him to his mother.' So Kay Khosrow went safely home to Farangis.",
      'Years went by. In Iran, old Goudarz dreamed that the angel Sorush told him: only your son Giv can find Kay Khosrow, the new king. So Giv set out alone, with just his horse and his lasso.',
      "He searched for seven long years. He ate what he could hunt and wore its skins, and his horse ate grass and drank salty water. At last he began to lose hope. 'Perhaps the prince was never born,' he said.",
      "Then one day he saw a young man by a shining spring, tall as a cypress, with a cup of water in his hand and flowers in his hair. Kay Khosrow saw Giv too, and smiled. 'You are Giv, son of Goudarz,' he said. 'My mother told me, from my father, that you would come to take me home.' 'Show me your sign,' said Giv. Kay Khosrow bared his arm: there was the black birthmark of his royal family. Giv knelt and wept for joy.",
      'One more friend was waiting: Behzad, his father’s black horse, who had been set free in the hills to wait for Kay Khosrow. When the horses came down to drink, Kay Khosrow went to the stream with his father’s saddle. Behzad saw it, sighed deeply, and stood quite still. Kay Khosrow stroked his face, saddled him and climbed on. Then he, his mother and Giv set off for Iran.',
    ],
