    paragraphs: [
      "One spring morning, King Kay Khosrow sat in his garden with his heroes. Suddenly a herdsman came running. 'A wild ass has come among our horses!' he cried. 'It is the colour of the sun, with a black stripe from its mane to its tail, and it is as strong as a lion. It is frightening the whole herd!'",
      "The king knew that no wild ass could frighten horses like that. 'This is something else,' he said. 'Rostam, go and see, and take care.' 'With your good fortune,' said Rostam, 'I am afraid of nothing.'",
      "Rostam rode out on Rakhsh. For three days he searched. On the fourth day he saw it, golden and shining, running like the north wind. 'I won't hurt it,' thought Rostam. 'I will catch it alive and bring it to the king.' He threw his lasso, and the wild ass vanished. Gone! 'That is no wild ass,' said Rostam. 'That is Akvan the div!' Divs are the strong, tricky creatures of the old tales.",
      'For three more days and nights Rostam chased it, until he was hungry and thirsty and nodding in the saddle. At last he came to a spring, sweet as rosewater. He gave Rakhsh a drink, laid the saddle under his head for a pillow, and fell fast asleep.',
      "Akvan saw him sleeping. He came like the wind, cut out the ground all around him, and lifted Rostam, ground and all, high up into the sky. Rostam woke up in the air! 'Make a wish,' said Akvan. 'Where shall I throw you? Into the sea, or onto the mountains?'",
      "Rostam thought fast. 'If I fall on the hard mountains, that will be the end of me. The sea would be better. But if I ask for the sea, he will throw me on the mountains. A div always does the opposite.'",
      "So Rostam said, 'Oh, not the sea! Throw me on the mountains, where the lions and tigers can see how strong I am!' Akvan roared, 'Then I will throw you where no one will ever find you!' And he threw Rostam into the deep sea. Splash!",
      'That was just what Rostam wanted. Big fish came swimming at him, but he drew his sword, and they swam away. He swam with one arm and both legs, and kept the fish away with the other arm, until he reached the shore. He climbed out, thanked God, and spread out his tiger-skin coat to dry.',
      'Then he walked back to the spring. Rakhsh was gone! Rostam picked up his saddle and bridle and followed the hoofprints until evening. There was Rakhsh, in a green meadow, grazing among a herd of horses. Rostam caught him with his lasso, brushed off the dust, saddled him and climbed on.',
      "Back at the spring, Akvan appeared again. 'Haven't you had enough?' he said. 'You got out of the sea, and you are back for more?' Rostam roared like a lion. He threw his lasso and caught the div, and he beat him with his heavy mace. Akvan was beaten, and he never troubled anyone again.",
      'Then Rostam rode home on Rakhsh, and King Kay Khosrow came out to meet him with banners and trumpets and drums.',
    ],
